Drop the truthiness tests on a va_list, which do not compile on aarch64.

va_list is an array type on aarch64, so testing one in a boolean context
is an array-to-pointer decay GCC rejects outright:

    src/log.c:124:22: error: invalid operands to binary && (have 'int' and 'va_list')
    lib/alex-c/src/str.c:11:13: error: wrong type argument to unary exclamation mark
    lib/alex-c/src/str.c:58:13: error: wrong type argument to unary exclamation mark

On x86_64 va_list is a pointer, the tests are vacuously true, and it
builds - which is why upstream has not noticed.

The tests were never meaningful. A va_list obtained from va_start is
always valid, every caller of these functions passes one, and there is no
portable null va_list to compare against in the first place. In log.c
__format alone is the real guard; in str.c the guard goes entirely, since
__format is checked by vsnprintf itself.

Worth reporting upstream. Until then the aarch64 leg of our build fails
here and way-displays is absent from the aarch64 repository.

--- a/src/log.c
+++ b/src/log.c
@@ -121,7 +121,7 @@
 	char *l;
 
 	// allocate the line for output and capture
-	if (__format && __args)
+	if (__format)
 		l = vsnprintf_alloc(MAX_LINE_LEN, __format, __args);
 	else
 		l = strdup("");
--- a/lib/alex-c/src/str.c
+++ b/lib/alex-c/src/str.c
@@ -8,9 +8,6 @@
 #include "str.h"
 
